# Sprint 28a (1b, M13) — the aborts macOS is ALLOWED to have, by REASON.
#
# Each line is a substring; every abort the suite reports must match one.
# An abort matching nothing FAILS the job.
#
# PROVENANCE: the Gradle line is MEASURED (CI run 31852349674). The other two
# are derived from the source's assumption messages — that run never reached
# the comparison on macOS, because the gate script itself died there on
# `mapfile`, which bash 3.2 does not have. Once fixed, the next run is what
# confirms this list.

# The embedding corpus is a large artifact not carried in the repository.
no corpus at -Djawata.embed.corpus

# The profiler ranks SAMPLED measurements; under contention it can return too
# few samples to rank anything, and refuses to assert on a coin toss.
CPU profile too thin

# The matrix jobs deliberately set -Djawata.skip.gradle=true; the separate
# gradle-cell job is where these must NOT skip. See expected-aborts.linux for
# the full reasoning.
Gradle Tooling API tests skipped via jawata.skip.gradle=true

# --- MEASURED on the first macOS run that reached the comparison (CI 31853736031) ---

# Native crash triage correlates symbols in the ELF/gdb dialect. macOS uses
# Mach-O and lldb, where the same symbols carry a leading underscore, and the
# test says so rather than asserting against a correlation it cannot perform.
# The dialect is DEFERRED to the Sprint-28-family native-triage batch — the
# design names it as placed-not-built, and the boundary already holds its
# seams (GdbAdapter keeps the debugger dialect, HostProcesses the spawn).
Mach-O/lldb unimplemented

# The fork census re-derives Stage 3's claims about the java-design-patterns
# corpus — which shapes occur in it, and how far its loop finder and loop
# rewriter disagree. The corpus is a CHECKOUT of somebody else's repository,
# nearly two thousand files, and it is deliberately not vendored here: the fork
# fixtures carry the slices we need, and copying the whole thing to make one
# test runnable would be worse than the test skipping. Where the checkout is
# absent the honest answer is "not measured", not "passed". Supplying
# -Djawata.test.fork=<path> turns it back on.
no fork checkout at

# mcp#26's DECLARING-PROJECT half. SourceIsAttributedToItsDeclarerTest asks which
# project a type is attributed to when one project RESOLVES a type another
# DECLARES — and it aborts rather than passing when the fixture cannot pose that
# question, in its own words: "a green line here would say 'the attribution is
# right' when what happened is that no project ever claimed a type it does not
# own, so the loop was never asked to choose." The kind="src"
# path="/xref-declarer" reference does not put the shared type on xref-consumer's
# classpath, so the condition never arises here.
#
# Acceptable because refusing an unearned green is the behaviour we want, and the
# SIBLING case in the same class DOES run, covering the issue's other half (the
# payload naming the declaring FILE). The cost is stated rather than hidden: on
# this fixture the declaring-project assertion is unverified.
#
# Budgeted at Sprint 28e Stage 5 (mcp#45). It had been aborting in every local run
# and nothing reported it, because run-suite.sh never called this gate — which is
# exactly the defect mcp#45 filed.
[mcp#26 ATTRIBUTION] NOT RUN
